or me maybe that's  how i like my language to be i don't  want or need to be judged by a third  party so i don't put that pressure on  myself so i do what i like to do  i do it when i like to do it  um and if i was really fed up i wouldn't  do it but i have to admit i have this  pressure on me because it's the 90-day  challenge i got to try to put in the  time  i know from experience that if i put in  the time i will see the results so  that's the other thing i'm confident  that if i just put in the time  i will get there so i don't i'm not  you know demanding of myself you know  why aren't you doing better none of that  so uh  and i try to get to uh you know  activities that are gonna be enjoyable  at first it's difficult i have to admit  i'm still struggling to understand what  i would call meaningful content  in korean whereas non-meaningful  artificial learner play content i can  understand but the real stuff still very  difficult it's kind of frustrating i  keep hitting it day after day but  i don't mind  because i know that the reward will be  there  and the reward will be that i'm much  better not necessarily that i've  achieved some tremendous uh you know  level  so you know i think that's it don't put  pressure on yourself if you're really  fed up don't do it have a variety of  different ways that you can interact  with the language  um  basically that's it so yeah i don't  i haven't burned out yet  although i have to admit if it weren't  for the 90-day challenge i would be  giving myself days off  and that reminds me i'm at right near  the top of the list in terms of activity  index here at link in this 90-day  challenge i don't like that i would like  to be in the middle of the pack  i hope other people will pick up the  pace  so that at the end of it all we can all  report on how much we improved  so  there you have it that's my take on on  overload or burnout i hope radco this uh
 

